        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/author/thomase>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/author/warner-r>;
    dcterms:coverage "Tala K'frida";
    dcterms:creator <http://viaf.org/viaf/56622759>;
    dcterms:description "The ancient name of this fortified grain-house is known only from a Latin inscription describing its restoration by a governor of Mauretaniae Caesariensis in the mid 290s CE under Diocletain and his tetrarchic colleagues. The inscription's findspot, near a spring in Algeria's Kefrida Pass depression, is close to remains of Roman fortifications noticed by French scholars in the 19th century on either side of the pass. They presumably guarded a road that ran south from Muslubium, as well as water sources (the \"Aqua Frigida\" itself?) supplying Muslubium via a recently discovered aqueduct (Ouaret Ladjouze). The modern toponymic component \"K'frida\" presumably reflects the ancient \"Frigida\".";
    dcterms:modified "2025-10-30T21:22:05-04:00";
    dcterms:subject "dare:ancient=1",
