<https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/392373882> a <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/vocab#Place>;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "CENTCOM Iraq 084. Tell ed-Der (ancient: Sippar Amnanum)",
        "Tanret 2010 ",
        "Wikidata Sippar-Amnanum (Q7525818)",
        "Wikipedia (English) Sippar-Amnanum",
        "de Meyer 1971-1984 ",
        "de Meyer 1980 ";
    dcterms:contributor <https://pleiades.stoa.org/author/cjohansson>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/author/jbecker>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/author/jnovotny>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/author/mdurusu>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/author/rrattenborg>,
        <https://pleiades.stoa.org/author/thomase>;
    dcterms:creator <https://pleiades.stoa.org/author/gmckee>;
    dcterms:description "Sippar-Amnanum (modern Tell ed-Der; also referred to as Sippar-Annunītu) was a small city located 6 km northeast of Sippar and 63 km north of Babylon. The goddess Annunītu was the patron deity of this sister city of Sippar, and Eulmaš was its principal temple. An archive of ca. 2,000 cuneiform tablets dating the the reigns of several kings of the First Dynasty of Babylon was excavated in the house of Ur-Utu, the chief lamentation-priest of the goddess Annunītu. From several inscriptions of Nabonidus (r. 555–539 BC), Babylon’s last native king, certainly, Sippar-Amnanum (which he refers to as Sippar-Annunītu) was still occupied and its cult was still in use in the sixth century BC, although it had been (partially) destroyed by the seventh-century-BC Assyrian king Sennacherib (r. 704–681 BC).";
    dcterms:modified "2025-08-11T20:49:16-04:00";
    dcterms:subject "ANE",
        "Babylonian";
    dcterms:title "Sippar-Amnanum";
    cito:citesAsRelated <https://web.archive.org/web/20190509203736/https://www.cemml.colostate.edu/cultural/09476/iraqstart.html>,
        <https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q7525818>;
    cito:citesForInformation <http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/614895723>,
